[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Every scribe command still loads the graph itself

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SCRIBE-COMMANDS-LOAD-IN-PROCESS
TITLE: Every scribe command still loads the graph itself
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Every scribe command -- show, list, check, new, set, relate and the rest --
still loads the whole graph in its own process, about a second each. The
daemon serves the render and nothing else.

Pointing them at the socket is not the work. `scribe new` loads the graph
three times: at startup, again to resolve the grammar alias on the file it
just created, and again at the end inside finish() -> verify() to prove the
written file did not break the corpus. Run that same code inside the daemon
and the third load still happens, so the command costs what it costs today
with a socket added in the middle.

The workspace already replaces that third load -- parse only the file just
written, defer the whole-graph check to the next read -- but verify() is
called from finish(), which every writing command goes through. So the
commands move only once their verification is the workspace's rather than
their own, and that is a change to the existing CLI, not to transport.

The likely shape afterwards is one socket call carrying the command line,
dispatched inside the daemon against the held graph, since main() already
takes only a graph and a root.
<<<
